The preview server is running, but every attempt to capture a screenshot is timing out. The console logs show nothing, and the document reports that it is hidden (visibilityState = "hidden", hidden = true). This explains why the renderer isn’t drawing anything: when the tab is hidden, three‑js stops rendering to save resources, so my attempts to grab a frame never produce an image and the preview hangs.

I need a way to force rendering even when the page is hidden, so I can capture frames for testing or debugging. The simplest approach is to add a small developer‑only hook that can be called from the console (or via an eval) to manually render the scene and extract the canvas contents as a data URL. That way I can invoke it even if the page is hidden, and I can also capture a specific frame of the showcase animation.

Looking at the existing index.html, there is already a resize listener near the bottom of the file:

```js
window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  camera.aspect = window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight;
  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
});
```

I can append my dev hook right after this block. I’ll keep the existing code unchanged and just add a comment and a new global object `window.__taj` with two helper methods:

1. `snap(x, y, z, tx, ty, tz, w = 800)` – positions the camera, renders once, copies the renderer’s canvas into a temporary off‑screen canvas of width `w` (height scaled proportionally), and returns a JPEG data URL. This will let me capture arbitrary viewpoints.

2. `showcaseFrame(t, w = 800)` – steps the showcase animation to a specific time `t` (by setting `showcase.t` and calling `updateShowcase` for one frame), renders, and returns a JPEG data URL similarly. This is useful for capturing a frame from the showcase without needing to run the full animation loop.

I must ensure the new code is syntactically correct and does not interfere with existing variables. The methods will use the existing `camera`, `renderer`, `scene`, and `showcase` objects, which are already in scope. I’ll also set `showcase.playing` appropriately to avoid side effects.

